  "description": "It was discovered that Pillow could enter an infinite loop while\ndetermining the frame count of a MIC image. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, and\nUbuntu 22.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112390)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's PdfParser.get_value() could recurse\nwithout a limit while reading nested PDF objects. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS,\nUbuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 18.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-112391)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's rank-based filters could dereference\ninvalid image storage for a zero-size image. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a crash. (CVE-2026-112392)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow had a heap buffer overflow in\n_tiffWriteProc when saving compressed TIFF data. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to crash the process or execute arbitrary\ncode. (CVE-2026-112393)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's P;2L and P;4L raw decoders could\nread past the end of an input buffer. An attacker could possibly\nuse this issue to disclose heap memory or crash the process.\n(CVE-2026-112394)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's tiled-TIFF decoder could read past\nthe tile buffer while copying four-byte pixels. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to expose heap data or crash the process.\nThis issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu\n24.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 22.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112395)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow had a heap use-after-free when libtiff\nrejects a tag while encoding TIFF data to an in-memory buffer. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to crash the process or\nexecute arbitrary code. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10 and\nUbuntu 26.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112396)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's ImageMorph.MorphOp.apply() could\naccess memory before a row buffer for a zero-width image. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause a crash.\n(CVE-2026-112397)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's FlashPix JPEG-tile decoder could\nwrite decoded scanlines beyond the allocated tile buffer. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to corrupt heap memory and\nexecute arbitrary code. (CVE-2026-112398)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could allocate an APNG disposal\nbackground at the full canvas size before applying the\ndecompression-bomb check. An attacker could possibly use this\nissue to exhaust memory and cause a denial of service. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS,\nand Ubuntu 22.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112399)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could retain a rejected GIF frame\nsize after a decompression-bomb exception. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to exhaust memory and cause a denial of\nservice. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS,\nand Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112400)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow performed quadratic copying while\nparsing GIF comments. An attacker could possibly use this issue\nto cause a denial of service. This issue only affected Ubuntu\n26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, and\nUbuntu 20.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112401)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could double free image memory when\nImagingScaleAffine encounters a selective allocation failure. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to crash the process.\n(CVE-2026-112402)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's JPEG decoder could write a\nfull-width scanline into a buffer sized for a requested draft\nscale. An attacker could possibly use this issue to corrupt heap\nmemory or execute arbitrary code. (CVE-2026-112403)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's TIFF tile and RGBA decode paths\ncould read beyond row data produced by libtiff. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to expose heap data or crash the process.\nThis issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu\n24.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 22.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112404)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's Image.merge() could dereference a\nnull image8 plane. An attacker could possibly use this issue to\ncause a crash. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10 and Ubuntu\n26.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112405)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could enter an infinite loop while\nrewriting PDF pages. An attacker could possibly use this issue to\ncause a denial of service. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10,\nUbuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04\nLTS, and Ubuntu 18.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112406)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow performed quadratic copying when\nsaving a GIF with a long comment. An attacker could possibly use\nthis issue to cause a denial of service. This issue only affected\nUbuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-112891)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's MSP RLE decoder could expand a\nsmall file into an unbounded in-memory buffer. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to exhaust memory and cause a denial of\nservice. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS,\nUbuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, and Ubuntu\n18.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112892)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could free an invalid glyph pointer\nduring FreeType rendering with an active stroker. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to corrupt memory or crash the process.\nThis issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, and\nUbuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-112893)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow took quadratic time to reserve PDF\nobject identifiers when saving many frames. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS,\nUbuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 18.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-112894)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could exhaust resources while parsing\nan existing PDF during an append operation. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS,\nUbuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 18.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-112895)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's TIFF encoder could read beyond a\nColorMap buffer when retained TIFF metadata has a plane size\ninconsistent with BitsPerSample. An attacker could possibly use\nthis issue to disclose heap memory or crash the process.\n(CVE-2026-112896)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could return uninitialized memory as\npixels when libtiff performs a short decode of JPEG-compressed or\nGroup 4 fax TIFF data. An attacker could possibly use this issue\nto disclose stale heap contents. This issue only affected Ubuntu\n26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 22.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-112897)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could disclose uninitialized stack\nmemory when Image.quantize() uses an empty palette. An attacker\ncould possibly use this issue to obtain stack contents. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 24.04\nLTS. (CVE-2026-112898)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow was vulnerable to argument injection\nwhen invoking Ghostscript for EPS input. An attacker could possibly\nuse this issue to inject Ghostscript arguments and execute\narbitrary commands. (CVE-2026-112900)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's XBM header parser was vulnerable\nto regular-expression denial of service. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service.\n(CVE-2026-112901)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could export non-contiguous image\ndata to Arrow arrays. An attacker could possibly use this issue to\ndisclose uninitialized memory or stale heap contents. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10 and Ubuntu 26.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-114501)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could overflow an integer when\ntracking glyph positions for very long strings. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause logic errors or resource\nconsumption. This issue only affected Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu\n22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, Ubuntu 18.04 LTS, Ubuntu 16.04 LTS,\nand Ubuntu 14.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-42308)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could hang indefinitely when\nprocessing a malicious PDF with a trailer loop. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause a denial of service. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04\nLTS, and Ubuntu 18.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-42310)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow did not properly apply decompression\nbomb checks after seeking to new frames in MPO, DCX, and MIC\nimages. An attacker could possibly use this issue to bypass the\ncheck and cause memory exhaustion. This issue only affected\nUbuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04\nLTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, Ubuntu 18.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 16.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2026-96636)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow did not properly apply decompression\nbomb checks after seeking to new frames in SPIDER images. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause memory exhaustion.\nThis issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu\n24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, Ubuntu 18.04 LTS,\nand Ubuntu 16.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-96637)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's BMP RLE decoder could expand a\nsmall file into an unbounded in-memory buffer. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to exhaust memory and cause a denial of\nservice. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS,\nand Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-96638)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's multi-frame loaders could retain\na stale ImagingCore allocation. An attacker could possibly use\nthis issue to cause out-of-bounds writes and crashes. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 26.10 and Ubuntu 26.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-96639)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow did not properly detect cyclic page\ntree references in PDF parsing. An attacker could possibly use\nthis issue to cause a denial of service. This issue only affected\nUbuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS,\nUbuntu 20.04 LTS, and Ubuntu 18.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-96640)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's PILfont text-width accumulation\ncould overflow due to unchecked negative glyph advances. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause logic errors or\nresource consumption. (CVE-2026-96641)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's TarIO initializer accepted a\nnegative octal member size. An attacker could possibly use this\nissue to cause an infinite loop and denial of service.\n(CVE-2026-96642)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow's Exif.load() could perform\nquadratic CPU work when processing repeated EXIF headers. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause a denial of\nservice. This issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10 and Ubuntu 26.04\nLTS. (CVE-2026-96643)\n\nIt was discovered that Pillow could call jpeg_write_marker() for a\nJPEG COM comment when an ICC profile has exactly filled the\n65536-byte encoder buffer. An attacker could possibly use this\nissue to cause a heap buffer overflow and execute arbitrary code.\nThis issue only affected Ubuntu 26.10, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, and\nUbuntu 24.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-96778)",
